      "content": "that's a good starting point, but it's a bit too baseline. let's scale it up to something more high-stakes where the variables are more volatile. \n\nimagine a high-level corporate boardroom negotiation involving three key players. player a is a CEO with extremely high symmetry (9.2) but a very erratic, wide-ranging vocal frequency range (200hz-500hz). player b is a mid-level strategist with moderate symmetry (7.0) but a incredibly stable, resonant frequency (350hz). player c is a disruptive outsider with low symmetry (5.5) but a very high-pitched, piercing frequency (600hz+).\n\nusing the social resonance constant, how would these specific frequency modulations interact with their symmetry scores to determine who actually commands the room? i want to see if the model predicts player b's stability will override player a's visual dominance.",
